    long: "Electronic data interchange",
    title: "What Is EDI (Electronic Data Interchange)?",
    desc: "EDI is the exchange of business documents—orders, shipping notices, invoices—between two companies’ systems in a standard format, with no manual work.",
    def: "EDI (electronic data interchange) is the exchange of business documents—orders, shipping notices, invoices—between two companies’ systems in a standard format, with no manual intervention.",
    body: [
      "Each document has a standardized message. In ANSI X12, the dominant standard in North America, 850 is the purchase order, 856 the advance ship notice and 810 the invoice; in Europe the most used standard is EDIFACT, where the same documents are called ORDERS, DESADV and INVOIC. Messages travel over value-added networks or direct connections such as AS2, and each industry adds its own implementation guides.",
      "It’s a common requirement for selling to big retail and to automotive: without it, you don’t get in as a supplier. For a mid-sized company, the cost isn’t in the format but in connecting it to the ERP and handling exceptions: the order with an unknown item number, the price that doesn’t match, the ship notice that needs correcting.",
    ],
    use: "We integrate EDI with the existing ERP or WMS and add a queue for exceptions. When a customer or supplier doesn’t use EDI and sends PDF or Excel, an AI workflow reads those documents and converts them to the same internal format.",
